  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simplified schematic of how a proton–proton collision can produce a top–antitop quark pair and how that event is identified from the particles it decays into. Two incoming protons collide, releasing many constituent quarks and gluons. A highlighted interaction shows two gluons fusing to create a top quark and an anti-top quark.\n\nThe top quark then decays into a W boson and a bottom quark, while the anti-top decays into an anti-bottom quark and another W-like decay chain. The W bosons subsequently decay into leptons: one side produces an anti-muon and a muon neutrino, and the other produces an electron and an anti-neutrino. The bottom and anti-bottom quarks emerge as additional visible products. Arrows indicate the sequence from the initial collision through intermediate particles to final detected particles.\n\nThe main message is that the short-lived top and anti-top are not seen directly; instead, their presence is reconstructed from their characteristic decay products. The diagram also emphasizes that angular information from the outgoing charged leptons is measured, suggesting these angles are important for studying the properties of the top-quark pair.",
